<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Catuvellauni</h1><p class="st-meta">54 BCE – 43 CE (approx.) · capital Verlamion (St Albans); later Camulodunum (Colchester)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading power of late Iron Age Britain, whose king Cunobelinus ruled much of the south-east and whose sons led resistance to the Roman invasion of 43.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Catuvellauni held the lands north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-thames">Thames</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hertfordshire">Hertfordshire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassivellaunus">Cassivellaunus</a>, who commanded British resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> in 54 BCE, is usually thought to have been their leader, with his stronghold perhaps at Wheathampstead. By the late 1st century BCE their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tasciovanus">Tasciovanus</a> was minting coins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verlamion</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-albans">St Albans</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their rulers issued abundant gold coinage, some styled with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> REX, and imported wine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and fine pottery from the Roman world, as rich burials at Welwyn and Folly Lane show. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunobelinus">Cunobelinus</a>, ruling from about 9 to 40 CE from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulodunum">Camulodunum</a>, which he took from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/trinovantes">Trinovantes</a>, extended influence over Kent and much of the south-east; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> called him king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britons-celtic-people">Britons</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunobelinus">Cunobelinus</a> died, his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togodumnus">Togodumnus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a> provoked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by attacking the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/atrebates-britain">Atrebates (Britain)</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> invaded in 43; Togodumnus was killed and Caratacus fled west to fight on among the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/silures">Silures</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ordovices">Ordovices</a> until 51. The tribe became the civitas Catuvellaunorum, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a>, in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/catuvellauni.jpg" alt="Catuvellauni" width="320" height="396"><figcaption>Image: Jpb1301 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Territory_of_the_Catuvellauni.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>54 BCE – 43 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Verlamion (St Albans); later Camulodunum (Colchester)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cassivellaunus, Tasciovanus, Cunobelinus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Cunobelinus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-6&amp;focus=catuvellauni" aria-label="Catuvellauni on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4681 387 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Catuvellauni lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5001 643L4975 655M5001 643L5020 640M5001 643L5304 942M5001 643L5020 640"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4975 655h0M5020 640h0M5304 942h0M5020 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M500